x86 mailbox ownership and quarantine
====================================

Run these host-side checks from the worktree:

  bash tools/testing/selftests/multikernel/mailbox_test.sh
  bash tools/testing/selftests/multikernel/mailbox_lifetime_test.sh
  bash tools/testing/selftests/multikernel/mailbox_reader_test.sh
  bash tools/testing/selftests/multikernel/pool_return_test.sh

The reader test requires x86-64 userspace and returns kselftest skip status 4
on other architectures. C checks also support CC=clang and sanitizer CFLAGS.

Protocol
--------

The existing 32-bit ready field carries these states, without moving fields:

  IDLE -> BUSY (publisher reservation) -> PENDING
       -> BUSY (reader claim) -> ACK -> IDLE (publisher consumption)

Every writer reserves the slot before changing dispatch fields. Cross-kernel
writers use this atomic reservation in addition to local park_lock ordering.
The reader's initial APIC comparison is only a filter: after PENDING -> BUSY
succeeds it re-reads the target and all dispatch data. A wrong-target reader
returns BUSY -> PENDING with compare/exchange. It cannot undo quarantine.

The reader stages all dispatch registers before BUSY -> ACK. It jumps only
if that compare/exchange succeeds. A publisher may reuse the dispatch fields
after consuming ACK, because no successful reader loads them afterward.
Atomic compare/exchange orders preceding stores before PENDING publication.

ACK is not proof that a CPU has left its old park code or page table. A repark
to another context still requires an independent self-repark acknowledgment
from the destination before old-context tracking can be removed.

The bounded wait is one second. After its final ACK-consumption attempt fails,
the publisher exchanges the state to QUARANTINED. There is no retry loop racing
wrong-target readers. A just-arrived ACK can lose this last race; the result is
conservative retention, not a false success. A reader that already ACKed may
still dispatch, so timeout never authorizes reclaiming its code or data.

Fail-closed scope
-----------------

Timeout quarantines the selected slot, this kernel's own context and its local
pool slot when present. A remote wake may still reference this kernel's
trampoline or pool page tables; an empty free-CPU set does not prove those
resources idle. This deliberately sacrifices further pool use after uncertainty.
A spawn's own-context marker is also visible to its parent.

No new writer reserves a quarantined slot. An already-reserved publisher can
finish stores after a concurrent quarantine, but its final compare/exchange
cannot republish or reset the marker. Resources remain pinned regardless.
Setup, spawn, confirmation, repark and pool teardown reject non-idle slots.
Instance teardown checks the context even if CPU tracking is empty.
Pool retirement also requires locally owned CPUs to be online: CPUHP may fail
after the mailbox ACK while the CPU still references pool code. This check
also conservatively blocks retirement for ordinary offline local CPUs until
they are onlined. It does not take CPUHP locks under the park lock; resource
serialization excludes new donations, and ordinary native offlining does not
send CPUs into pool memory.
Confirmation selects the recorded slot once; it does not retry on another
slot after timeout or repair uncertain tracking by guessing a location.

Recovery is a full host reboot. There is no sysfs reset, automatic retry,
generation reset, or old-peer compatibility mode. CPUHP rollback, absence
from a CPU set and a late shutdown ACK are not mailbox retirement proofs.

Compatibility and landing
-------------------------

This changes shared protocol semantics, even though context offsets and size
are unchanged. Host, spawn and nested kernels must all use the updated reader
and writers. Different configurations are permitted only with compatible
context layout and this same protocol; mixing old/new kernels is unsupported.
The C writers, assembly reader and shared state definitions must land together.

Evidence limits
---------------

mailbox_test extracts the actual writer/setup functions, using simulated poll
outcomes. mailbox_lifetime_test executes real release/teardown paths against
counted allocation boundaries, including empty tracking. mailbox_reader_test
executes actual primary/secondary reader instructions in userspace, with a
capture trampoline; it excludes CR3, descriptor setup, MONITOR/MWAIT and repark.
pool_return_test checks the actual pool retirement predicate with retained
offline or invalid local CPU ownership, despite empty free/child sets.
Ownership and repark-arrival fixtures mock the mailbox primitive and cannot
prove this protocol independently.

These checks and object builds are not live lockdep, native halt, physical CPU
retirement or end-to-end spawn qualification. Final-stack tests must use fresh
VMs for quarantined failure cases, and verify retention rather than in-place
recovery. Earlier v21 runtime results do not qualify this protocol change.
